1
00:00:00,026 --> 00:00:07,422
Skip Q is a food court ordering application with two roles: diner and stall vendor.
This video records the

2
00:00:07,422 --> 00:00:12,093
running application, using seeded demonstration accounts and simulated payments.
Browser actions are

3
00:00:12,093 --> 00:00:18,711
automated, and this voice is computer generated. I will follow one new order across
both roles, demonstrate

4
00:00:18,711 --> 00:00:22,993
two checkout refusals, and show the supporting features and project evidence.

5
00:00:23,746 --> 00:00:29,975
First, the diner signs in through the actual login form. The account enters the
protected diner area. The

6
00:00:29,975 --> 00:00:34,475
vendor will use a separate browser session, so the two roles remain independent.

7
00:00:35,232 --> 00:00:41,609
The test data includes a cart. I remove its existing lines through the interface, so
the new order in this

8
00:00:41,609 --> 00:00:43,522
demonstration starts from an empty cart.

9
00:00:44,288 --> 00:00:51,097
Only open stalls appear in the list. Charcoal Grill is open. Its menu shows prices,
descriptions, and images.

10
00:00:51,097 --> 00:00:55,636
Pineapple Tart is already sold out, and its Add button is disabled.

11
00:00:56,403 --> 00:01:03,519
I add one Charcoal Chicken Rice. The updated button confirms one is in the cart. The
cart shows the item,

12
00:01:03,519 --> 00:01:07,789
quantity, and total of six dollars fifty. The server calculates this total.

13
00:01:08,543 --> 00:01:14,557
Checkout offers Pay Now, Card, and Cashless simulations. I deliberately select the
payment failure control.

14
00:01:14,557 --> 00:01:21,774
The application displays a real payment refusal, retains the cart, and allows a safe
retry. This failure is

15
00:01:21,774 --> 00:01:23,378
kept in the recording.

16
00:01:24,131 --> 00:01:30,435
I turn the failure control off and retry on the same checkout screen. Payment
succeeds, creating one order and

17
00:01:30,435 --> 00:01:35,412
a unique queue number. This number will identify the same order on both roles'
screens.

18
00:01:36,165 --> 00:01:41,923
The tracking screen shows Pending and the purchased item. It polls every three
seconds for changes made by the

19
00:01:41,923 --> 00:01:42,226
vendor.

20
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:48,892
Now the vendor signs in through the login form in an independent session. The paid
order queue contains the

21
00:01:48,892 --> 00:01:51,381
exact queue number generated by the diner's checkout.

22
00:01:52,132 --> 00:01:57,791
The vendor opens that order and accepts it. Its status changes from Pending to
Preparing. The screen offers

23
00:01:57,791 --> 00:02:00,306
only the next actions allowed by the server.

24
00:02:01,061 --> 00:02:07,277
Back on the original tracking screen, the diner sees Preparing through its own
polling. There is no manual

25
00:02:07,277 --> 00:02:08,658
refresh or replacement order.

26
00:02:09,409 --> 00:02:15,205
The vendor marks the order ready for collection. A no show is permitted only thirty
minutes after the ready

27
00:02:15,205 --> 00:02:18,256
time. That action is unavailable for this newly ready order.

28
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:24,478
The diner sees the green collection banner after polling. It tells the diner to
collect the order at the

29
00:02:24,478 --> 00:02:24,766
counter.

30
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:31,904
The vendor marks the same order collected. It becomes final and read only. The diner
also observes Collected,

31
00:02:31,904 --> 00:02:33,323
with the completion message.

32
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:42,925
The order list separates All, Current, and Past. The completed demonstration order
is now in Past. An older

33
00:02:42,925 --> 00:02:49,382
seeded purchase still records Grilled Chicken Rice at six dollars, although today's
menu uses a different name

34
00:02:49,382 --> 00:02:52,421
and price. History keeps the original purchase snapshot.

35
00:02:53,175 --> 00:02:59,731
For the first rule violation, I add an available Mango Sago and open checkout for
two dollars fifty. I keep

36
00:02:59,731 --> 00:03:03,010
this checkout screen open while the vendor changes the item.

37
00:03:03,763 --> 00:03:10,019
The rule is that an unavailable item cannot be ordered. Before paying, the vendor
will mark this exact item

38
00:03:10,019 --> 00:03:16,604
sold out. I expect checkout to refuse it, name the unavailable item, retain the
cart, and create no new order.

39
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:23,563
The vendor marks Mango Sago sold out. The diner still has the checkout screen loaded
from before that change.

40
00:03:23,563 --> 00:03:25,522
This is a genuine stale screen.

41
00:03:26,276 --> 00:03:32,918
I press Pay on that existing screen. Checkout is refused and names Mango Sago. The
cart remains available for

42
00:03:32,918 --> 00:03:38,161
review. A read only verification of the stored orders confirms no additional order
was created.

43
00:03:38,915 --> 00:03:45,021
Reviewing the cart shows the same item with its sold out state. Known unavailable
lines block checkout, while

44
00:03:45,021 --> 00:03:47,734
Remove remains available so the diner can recover.

45
00:03:48,497 --> 00:03:54,773
I remove the sold out item, add one available Chicken Rice, and open checkout for
six dollars fifty while the

46
00:03:54,773 --> 00:03:56,028
stall is still open.

47
00:03:56,781 --> 00:04:03,504
The second rule is that a closed stall cannot accept a new order. Before paying, the
vendor will close

48
00:04:03,504 --> 00:04:09,519
Charcoal Grill. I expect a visible checkout refusal, the cart retained, and no
additional order. Existing paid

49
00:04:09,519 --> 00:04:10,934
orders must remain accessible.

50
00:04:11,687 --> 00:04:17,727
The vendor closes the stall. The closed banner explains that new checkouts are
refused and existing paid

51
00:04:17,727 --> 00:04:18,793
orders remain accessible.

52
00:04:19,547 --> 00:04:26,123
On the already open checkout, I press Pay. The application refuses the closed stall
and explains when to try

53
00:04:26,123 --> 00:04:31,314
again. The cart still contains the Chicken Rice, and the stored order count is
unchanged.

54
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:39,533
I reopen the stall and restock Mango Sago. The vendor can also add a menu item, edit
its price and

55
00:04:39,533 --> 00:04:45,507
description, then remove it. The form states its validation rules, and removal asks
for confirmation. This

56
00:04:45,507 --> 00:04:47,747
temporary demonstration item is removed afterwards.

57
00:04:48,499 --> 00:04:54,176
This is a separate seeded Pending order, not the collected demonstration order.
Rejecting it produces

58
00:04:54,176 --> 00:04:59,097
Cancelled and a refunded payment. The screen states the refund outcome before
confirmation.

59
00:04:59,849 --> 00:05:05,620
This separate seeded order has been ready for more than thirty minutes. The
permitted no show action now

60
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:11,392
appears. Confirming it produces No Show and keeps the payment paid. No timestamp is
changed to bypass the

61
00:05:11,392 --> 00:05:11,712
rule.

62
00:05:12,779 --> 00:05:20,412
The project uses React for the interface, Flask for the API, and Mongo D B for
persistent data. The retained

63
00:05:20,412 --> 00:05:26,137
October seventh checks show three hundred thirteen unit tests passed, ten real
database functional tests

64
00:05:26,137 --> 00:05:31,862
passed twice, and the assessed browser lifecycle passed twice. Production build and
thirty layout captures

65
00:05:31,862 --> 00:05:36,824
were also checked. These are recorded results, not tests rerun during this video.

66
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:44,031
The recorded load measurement used ten simulated users for two minutes: three
hundred ninety five menu

67
00:05:44,031 --> 00:05:50,885
requests, zero failures, and a menu response ninety fifth percentile of twenty
milliseconds. This is a small

68
00:05:50,885 --> 00:05:57,336
local seeded dataset on the development server. It does not establish production
capacity or isolate database

69
00:05:57,336 --> 00:05:58,142
execution time.

70
00:05:58,894 --> 00:06:06,029
This live recording showed diner and vendor sign in, one order through Pending,
Preparing, Ready, and

71
00:06:06,029 --> 00:06:12,718
Collected, and both announced rule violations refused visibly. It also showed
payment retry, history, menu

72
00:06:12,718 --> 00:06:19,407
management, refund, and no show. The video uses computer generated narration and
automated browser actions.

73
00:06:19,407 --> 00:06:24,759
Review it before submission. Publishing, marker access, and submission remain
separate steps.
